e-ordering or descoping epics.\" HLR-3 should be a separate P1 epic owned by\nthe same team, sequenced after (or in parallel with) the P0 side-by-side work,\nwith the shared-surface concern handled as an AC/coordination gate rather than\na reason to fuse priorities.\n\n**Major issue: priority collapse — deferrable P1 feature (HLR-3) bundled into\nP0 epic E001; independent deferral is impossible per the decomposition's own\nHealth Warning 6.**\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nNo circular dependencies. Every edge in the mermaid diagram matches the target\nepic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E003: [E001, E002]; E004: [E001,\nE002, E003]; E001/E002: []), and arrows run dependency → dependent per the\ndiagram convention. Each edge is justified: E001→E003 and E002→E003 are\nconsumer edges against the comparison view and the run-reference data artifact\nrespectively; the three edges into E004 are valid Rule 11 docs edges. E001 and\nE002 (different repos, no shared artifacts) correctly run in parallel and\nconverge at E003 — no unjustified serialization. No konflux/repo/license\nepics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. No\nbranch files exist. Critical path length 3 (E001/E002 → E003 → E004) is\nappropriate for a strategy of this size (S/M standard: 3-4). No uncaptured\ndata/artifact dependencies found in epic content: E003's consumption of E002's\nREST API exposure and E001's comparison view are both edged.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: E001/E003 (Dashboard / RHOAI\nDashboard), E002 (Model Registry / Model Registry), E004 (Documentation /\nRHOAI Documentation). No epic spans multiple components or teams. No scope\nduplication: E003 consumes rather than rebuilds the comparison view (its scope\nis the registry entry point and seeding), and E002 stores the run reference\nthat E003 displays — the HLR-2 split along the storage/UI axis is drawn\ncorrectly. Work-product types are not mixed: content authoring (E004) is\nseparate from all code epics. Epic sizes are within a single sprint each.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll four epics are Implementation and all produce concrete artifacts (UI\ncapability, registry metadata + API exposure, UI entry point, documentation\ntopics). No Investigation epics were created, which is correct: the strategy\ndeclares \"Open Questions: None\", and the one unclear passage (registration-time\nrun-reference stamping) is scoped inside E002 as an implementation decision\nwith the residual DSP ambiguity properly disclosed as a non-blocking health\nwarning rather than silently dropped. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\nfields are null, consistent with the absence of gates. `implementation_type:\ndocs-authoring` on E004 is correctly applied.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nAll epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not\n`investigation_signals`); all four ai-signals rationale files exist and their\nrationales match the frontmatter values. E001, E003, and E004 signal sets are\nconsistent with the strategy content and well justified (notably E004's\n`human_process_gates: 0` and `repo_access: 0` for the Red Hat docs content\nprocess).\n\nOne arguable value: E002's `external_dependency: 0` is self-contradictory.\nIts own rationale concedes the epic \"uses the existing extension point\" while\nscoring 0 on the grounds that upstream kubeflow/model-registry coordination\n\"may\" be needed — but the epic's scope and its `adapter_pattern: 1` /\n`pattern_precedent: 1` rationales state the change stays within the documented\ncustom-properties extension point, which implies no upstream coordination.\nThe coordination risk is speculative, not established by the strategy or the\narchitecture context, so 1 appears to be the rubric-consistent value.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria derived from the\nstrategy. Rule-mandat