{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8408d5b56c82e7e6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9015\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n9\t  description: 'Priority collapse in E001: HLR-3 (P1, run diff view) is a distinct\n10\t    deferrable feature bundled into the P0 side-by-side comparison epic. The stated\n11\t    justification (shared UI surface and data) is boundary convenience, which the\n12\t    independent-deferral planning requirement overrides; the decomposition''s own\n13\t    Health Warning 6 admits planners cannot defer HLR-3 independently. Split HLR-3\n14\t    into a separate P1 epic for RHOAI Dashboard and handle the shared surface as a\n15\t    coordination/AC gate.'\n16\t- severity: minor\n17\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n18\t  description: E002 external_dependency=0 contradicts the epic's own scope and adapter_pattern/pattern_precedent\n19\t    rationales, which state the change stays within the documented custom-properties\n20\t    extension point; the upstream kubeflow/model-registry coordination risk is speculative,\n21\t    so 1 is the rubric-consistent value.\n22\terror: null\n23\t---\n24\t## Review Summary\n25\t\n26\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n27\tRecommendation: revise\n28\t\n29\tThe DAG, boundaries, types, acceptance criteria, and completeness are all\n30\tsound. The decomposition fails on one dimension: HLR-3 (P1, run diff view) is\n31\tbundled into the P0 epic E001, collapsing a deferrable P1 feature into P0 work\n32\tin violation of the priority-split planning requirement.\n33\t\n34\t## Criterion Details\n35\t\n36\t### 1. HLR Coverage (0/2)\n37\t\n38\tAll four HLRs are mapped and the traceability matrix is present and accurate\n39\t(HLR-1→E001, HLR-2→E002+E003, HLR-3→E001, HLR-4→E004). Priority inheritance\n40\tis otherwise correct: E001 is P0 (delivers HLR-1 directly), E002/E003 are P1,\n41\tand E004's P1 correctly derives from the docs-authoring exception\n42\t(strategy priority Major → P1), not from HLR-4's P2 level.\n43\t\n44\tHowever, E001 maps to HLRs at two priority levels: HLR-1 (P0, side-by-side\n45\tview) and HLR-3 (P1, run diff view). HLR-3 is a distinct deferrable feature,\n46\tnot incidental polish on the P0 work: it is a first-class requirement with its\n47\town HLR ID and its own acceptance criteria, delivers separate user value\n48\t(highlighting changed parameters/metrics between two runs), and the P0\n49\tside-by-side capability is fully deliverable without it. The decomposition's\n50\tjustification for bundling — that the diff view \"shares the same UI surface\n51\tand the same parameter/metric data\" and splitting \"would force two epics to\n52\tbuild the same surface\" — is boundary convenience, which the planning\n53\trequirement explicitly overrides. The decomposition's own Health Warning 6\n54\tadmits the harm: \"Planners cannot defer HLR-3 independently of the P0 work by\n55\tre-ordering or descoping epics.\" HLR-3 should be a separate P1 epic owned by\n56\tthe same team, sequenced after (or in parallel with) the P0 side-by-side work,\n57\twith the shared-surface concern handled as an AC/coordination gate rather than\n58\ta reason to fuse priorities.\n59\t\n60\t**Major issue: priority collapse — deferrable P1 feature (HLR-3) bundled into\n61\tP0 epic E001; independent deferral is impossible per the decomposition's own\n62\tHealth Warning 6.**\n63\t\n64\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n65\t\n66\tNo circular dependencies. Every edge in the mermaid diagram matches the target\n67\tepic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E003: [E001, E002]; E004: [E001,\n68\tE002, E003]; E001/E002: []), and arrows run dependency → dependent per the\n69\tdiagram convention. Each edge is justified: E001→E003 and E002→E003 are\n70\tconsumer edges against the comparison view and the run-reference data artifact\n71\trespectively; the three edges into E004 are valid Rule 11 docs edges. E001 and\n72\tE002 (different repos, no shared artifacts) correctly run in parallel and\n73\tconverge at E003 — no unjustified serialization. No konflux/repo/license\n74\tepics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. No\n75\tbranch files exist. Critical path length 3 (E001/E002 → E003 → E004) is\n76\tappropriate for a strategy of this size (S/M standard: 3-4). No uncaptured\n77\tdata/artifact dependencies found in epic content: E003's consumption of E002's\n78\tREST API exposure and E001's comparison view are both edged.\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n81\t\n82\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: E001/E003 (Dashboard / RHOAI\n83\tDashboard), E002 (Model Registry / Model Registry), E004 (Documentation /\n84\tRHOAI Documentation). No epic spans multiple components or teams. No scope\n85\tduplication: E003 consumes rather than rebuilds the comparison view (its scope\n86\tis the registry entry point and seeding), and E002 stores the run reference\n87\tthat E003 displays — the HLR-2 split along the storage/UI axis is drawn\n88\tcorrectly. Work-product types are not mixed: content authoring (E004) is\n89\tseparate from all code epics. Epic sizes are within a single sprint each.\n90\t\n91\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n92\t\n93\tAll four epics are Implementation and all produce concrete artifacts (UI\n94\tcapability, registry metadata + API exposure, UI entry point, documentation\n95\ttopics). No Investigation epics were created, which is correct: the strategy\n96\tdeclares \"Open Questions: None\", and the one unclear passage (registration-time\n97\trun-reference stamping) is scoped inside E002 as an implementation decision\n98\twith the residual DSP ambiguity properly disclosed as a non-blocking health\n99\twarning rather than silently dropped. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\n100\tfields are null, consistent with the absence of gates. `implementation_type:\n101\tdocs-authoring` on E004 is correctly applied.\n102\t\n103\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n104\t\n105\tAll epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not\n106\t`investigation_signals`); all four ai-signals rationale files exist and their\n107\trationales match the frontmatter values. E001, E003, and E004 signal sets are\n108\tconsistent with the strategy content and well justified (notably E004's\n109\t`human_process_gates: 0` and `repo_access: 0` for the Red Hat docs content\n110\tprocess).\n111\t\n112\tOne arguable value: E002's `external_dependency: 0` is self-contradictory.\n113\tIts own rationale concedes the epic \"uses the existing extension point\" while\n114\tscoring 0 on the grounds that upstream kubeflow/model-registry coordination\n115\t\"may\" be needed — but the epic's scope and its `adapter_pattern: 1` /\n116\t`pattern_precedent: 1` rationales state the change stays within the documented\n117\tcustom-properties extension point, which implies no upstream coordination.\n118\tThe coordination risk is speculative, not established by the strategy or the\n119\tarchitecture context, so 1 appears to be the rubric-consistent value.\n120\t\n121\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n122\t\n123\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria derived from the\n124\tstrategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E004\n125\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implemented-behavior AC; no\n126\tepic is a replacement (all greenfield features), so rollback/feature-flag ACs\n127\tare not required; no konflux chain exists, so no build-pipeline AC applies.\n128\tE001 includes the passive-component validation AC for Data Science Pipelines\n129\tand the out-of-scope guard (single project, no auto-promotion). E003 includes\n130\tgraceful degradation for model versions without a run reference. No epic body\n131\treferences sibling epics by draft ID — dependencies are described by\n132\tcapability, not by \"E00x\" labels.\n133\t\n134\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n135\t\n136\tAll strategy scope is covered: HLR-1 through HLR-4 map to epics, the Out of\n137\tScope list (auto best-run selection/promotion, cross-cluster/cross-project\n138\tcomparison) is respected and encoded as an AC in E001, and HLR-2's two halves\n139\t(storage/exposure in E002, UI entry in E003) are collectively accounted for.\n140\tData Science Pipelines is reasonably classified passive (no identifiable code\n141\tchange; consumption via existing KFP v2 interfaces, validated as an AC), with\n142\tthe residual registration-stamping ambiguity disclosed in Health Warning 2\n143\trather than silently dropped. The docs-authoring epic (Rule 11) is present,\n144\tmatching HLR-4. Strategy-level context — missing-sections caveats, the\n145\tcustomer-council wording overlap, and the DSP ambiguity — is carried forward\n146\tin health warnings rather than dropped. The documentation covers both entry\n147\tpoints (runs list and registry) as HLR-4 requires.\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab5e0f38c6e8aeee","agent_id":"aaaa44161d60520c4","session_id":"a66a1624-e5ea-4bd7-921c-fe9a2fc3883b","uuid":"49a4c2c4-0843-4918-be69-371623c1355c","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:17:11.025Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8408d5b56c82e7e6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Second revision of RHAISTRAT-9015"}
